{"context":"Every software project that deploys to a server must manage releases — packaging source code into artifacts, verifying integrity, activating on target environments, and recovering from failures. Despite being a universal concern, release management is typically implemented from scratch for each project.\n\nThe consequence is a proliferation of inconsistent release models:\n\n**Ad-hoc scripts.** Teams write shell scripts that copy files to servers. These scripts work on the author's machine but fail in CI. They have no state tracking, no rollback, and no verification.\n\n**CI workflow hacks.** Deployment logic is embedded in CI platform YAML using platform-specific syntax. The logic is unreusable across projects and untestable locally.\n\n**Framework-bound tools.** Framework-specific deployment too Source: ADR-003-runtime-and-release-lifecycle.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:Accepted.","decision":"Anvil adopts a deterministic lifecycle model for all software releases.\n\nEvery release managed by Anvil MUST progress through a predefined sequence of lifecycle stages.\n\nThe lifecycle is:\n\n- **Deterministic** — the progression is defined and predictable\n- **Explicit** — every transition is a deliberate action\n- **Observable** — the current stage of any release is always knowable\n- **Enforced** — undefined transitions are not permitted\n\nThe lifecycle belongs to the Core. It is the same for every project, every framework, and every environment. Framework adapters participate during execution but never redefine the lifecycle itself. Applications consume the lifecycle but never redefine its semantics.","consequences":"### 10.1 Positive Consequences\n\n**Predictable deployments.** Every release follows the same lifecycle. Teams know exactly what to expect at each stage. Automation can rely on consistent behavior.\n\n**Safer rollback.** Rollback is a first-class lifecycle transition with defined semantics. Teams do not need to invent rollback procedures for each release.\n\n**Consistent runtime state.** The invariant that only one release is active at a time eliminates ambiguity about what is running in each environment.\n\n**Portable release model.** The lifecycle is the same across projects, frameworks, and environments. A developer who understands Anvil's lifecycle understands any project that uses Anvil.\n\n**Stable extension points.** Framework adapters implement phases that execute within a fixed lifecycle. T","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n### 11.1 Mutable Releases\n\n**Description:** Releases can be modified after packaging. If a problem is found, the artifact is patched and re-deployed without creating a new release.\n\n**Rejected because:** Mutable releases violate the principle of verification integrity. What was verified is not what runs in production. Patching releases in-place makes it impossible to know which version of the code is actually deployed and introduces an unreliable deployment process.\n\n### 11.2 Framewo"}